"<p>Deploy your own web apps, APIs, and static sites directly from any Git repository (GitHub, GitLab, Gitea/Forgejo, or custom Git servers) with automatic builds on push.</p>\n<h2>Supported Builders</h2>\n<p>Nitrobase can detect your builder automatically during wizard inspection (<strong>Check repository</strong>) or let you choose explicitly:</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n<table><thead><tr><th align=\"left\">Builder</th><th align=\"center\">Default Port</th><th align=\"left\">How It Works</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Dockerfile</strong></td><td align=\"center\"><code>3000</code></td><td align=\"left\">Builds the <code>Dockerfile</code> in your repository with configurable Dockerfile path, build target, and build arguments.</td></tr><tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Railpack</strong></td><td align=\"center\"><code>3000</code></td><td align=\"left\">Detects your language and framework (Node, Python, Go, PHP, Ruby, Java, Rust, etc.) using <a href=\"https://railpack.com\">Railpack</a> (<code>v0.40.1</code> + BuildKit frontend) and builds a production container image with no <code>Dockerfile</code> required.</td></tr><tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Static site</strong></td><td align=\"center\"><code>80</code></td><td align=\"left\">Runs your build command in a build container (<code>node:22-alpine</code> by default) and serves the output folder with a lightweight static web server (<code>joseluisq/static-web-server</code>).</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Deploying from Git</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Go to <strong>Apps → New App → From Git repository</strong>.</li>\n<li>Enter your repository URL (HTTPS or SSH). For private repositories over SSH, Nitrobase can generate a dedicated <strong>Deploy Key</strong> to add to your Git host.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Check repository</strong> to inspect branches and auto-detect the builder.</li>\n<li>Select the target <strong>Branch</strong>, <strong>Builder</strong>, exposed container port, environment variables, and optional custom domain.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Deploy</strong>. Nitrobase clones the branch in an isolated throwaway container (<code>alpine/git</code>), builds the image via a dedicated per-server BuildKit builder (<code>nitrobase-builder</code>) with configurable memory/CPU/timeout limits (default <code>2048 MB</code> RAM, <code>2</code> CPUs, <code>20 min</code> timeout), and rolls out the new container with health checks.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Push Webhooks (Auto-Deploy on Push)</h2>\n<p>Each Git app can enable a <strong>Push Webhook</strong> (<code>/api/hooks/git/&#x3C;hookId></code>) supporting GitHub, GitLab, Gitea/Forgejo, and generic HMAC-signed webhooks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Debounce:</strong> Rapid pushes are debounced (<code>WEBHOOK_DEBOUNCE_MS</code>, default <code>10s</code>) so only the latest commit builds.</li>\n<li><strong>Replay &#x26; Rate Protection:</strong> Delivery IDs are checked against replays (7-day TTL) and rate-limited (<code>30</code> requests/min per IP and hook).</li>\n<li><strong>Build History &#x26; Rollback:</strong> Nitrobase keeps the last <code>5</code> successful images per app by default (<code>GIT_KEEP_IMAGES</code>, configurable up to <code>50</code>) so you can roll back to a previous commit quickly.</li>\n<li><strong>Cache Pruning:</strong> BuildKit cache is automatically pruned after each build to stay within <code>BUILD_CACHE_KEEP_MB</code> (default <code>4096 MB</code>).</li>\n</ul>"